## 3. Authentication, Device Detection & Session Flow

The platform secures user data using blind-index lookups with PII encryption (`user_email_enc`, `user_name_enc`), detects client device types (Normal Browser vs VR Headset), maintains active server-side sessions, and issues signed JWT access and refresh tokens.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor User as Trainee / Instructor / Admin
    participant Client as Client App (Web / VR)
    participant Middleware as Device & Tracking Middleware
    participant AuthRouter as Auth Controller (/login)
    participant DB as PostgreSQL (user_data)
    participant SessionStore as Session Store & Crypto

    User->>Client: Enter Email & Password
    Client->>Middleware: POST /api/v1/login (with Headers & Body)
    Middleware->>Middleware: Detect Device (Headers / User-Agent: VR vs Browser)
    Middleware->>AuthRouter: Forward Request + deviceInfo
    AuthRouter->>AuthRouter: Normalize Email & Compute blind index (emailLookup)
    AuthRouter->>DB: SELECT user_email_enc, user_name_enc, user_password, user_role, status WHERE user_email_lookup = $1
    DB-->>AuthRouter: Return Encrypted User Row
    
    alt User not found or Inactive
        AuthRouter-->>Client: 404 Not Found / Account Disabled
    else User Found
        AuthRouter->>AuthRouter: Compare Passwords (bcrypt / argon2)
        alt Password Mismatch
            AuthRouter-->>Client: 401 Invalid Password
        else Password Valid
            AuthRouter->>SessionStore: Decrypt PII (decryptPii for email & name)
            AuthRouter->>SessionStore: createSession(user, deviceInfo, ip)
            SessionStore->>DB: INSERT INTO user_sessions (session_id, user_email, device, login_source)
            AuthRouter->>AuthRouter: Generate JWT accessToken & refreshToken (includes sid, role, isVR)
            AuthRouter->>DB: Log Login Attempt (LoginAttemptModel)
            AuthRouter-->>Client: 200 OK + Tokens + User Profile + HttpOnly Cookie
        end
    end
```

